WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.000
The closed eye lost the cortex, though its retina still worked.

2
00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:09.380
Was that disuse, or competition?

3
00:00:10.533 --> 00:00:13.893
Disuse says connections that carry little signal wither.

4
00:00:14.753 --> 00:00:17.433
On this reading, an eye's fate depends on its own use.

5
00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:22.560
Competition says the two eyes compete for influence over each shared cell.

6
00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:25.540
One eye's advantage costs the other its share.

7
00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:30.660
On this reading, an eye's fate depends on its use compared with the other eye's.

8
00:00:31.683 --> 00:00:34.903
For one closed eye, both ideas predict the same loss.

9
00:00:35.623 --> 00:00:37.783
So that experiment cannot tell them apart.

10
00:00:38.783 --> 00:00:41.083
They part company when both eyes are closed.

11
00:00:42.083 --> 00:00:44.323
Some toy bookkeeping makes this concrete.

12
00:00:45.203 --> 00:00:47.203
The numbers are illustrative, not measured.

13
00:00:48.217 --> 00:00:52.797
Give an open eye 10 units of useful activity, and a sutured eye 1 unit.

14
00:00:53.717 --> 00:00:57.917
With both eyes open, disuse says each eye keeps all of its own connections.

15
00:00:59.277 --> 00:01:01.077
Competition gives each eye half the influence.

16
00:01:02.117 --> 00:01:07.417
Close one eye. Under disuse, it keeps only what its own use supports, a tenth.

17
00:01:08.297 --> 00:01:10.777
Under competition, its share shrinks to a sliver.

18
00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:17.580
Now close both. Under disuse, each eye is as unused as the single closed eye was.

19
00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:21.570
Under competition, neither eye has an advantage.

20
00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:26.790
Predict. Close both eyes of young kittens for the same weeks.

21
00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:30.190
What does each idea expect the histogram to show?

22
00:01:36.583 --> 00:01:41.403
Disuse expects weak responses to both eyes, and perhaps many silent cells.

23
00:01:42.803 --> 00:01:44.423
Competition expects the balance to hold.

24
00:01:45.367 --> 00:01:48.867
Wiesel and Hubel did this experiment in 1965.

25
00:01:50.150 --> 00:01:52.550
They scored many cells on the seven-group scale.

26
00:01:53.450 --> 00:01:56.970
They also counted cells that responded abnormally, or not at all.

27
00:01:57.933 --> 00:02:00.013
Many cells still responded to each eye.

28
00:02:00.853 --> 00:02:03.373
The balance moved far less than after one-eye closure.

29
00:02:07.866 --> 00:02:11.106
A share of cells did respond abnormally or not at all.

30
00:02:11.906 --> 00:02:13.886
So going without vision is not harmless.

31
00:02:14.916 --> 00:02:17.796
But the large loss that disuse predicts was absent.

32
00:02:18.850 --> 00:02:23.450
What cost the single closed eye its cells was mainly the open eye's advantage.

33
00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:26.240
That is the first part of the rule.

34
00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:29.100
The two eyes compete for the cells they share.

35
00:02:30.083 --> 00:02:31.623
One warning about the table.

36
00:02:32.463 --> 00:02:33.523
It is only bookkeeping.

37
00:02:34.566 --> 00:02:36.686
Models of competition add feedback.

38
00:02:37.566 --> 00:02:39.626
An eye that gets ahead is strengthened further.

39
00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:45.600
So inside the window, a strong imbalance can move most cells, not a fixed fraction

40
00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:46.600
of them.

41
00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:49.040
The rule gives the direction of change.

42
00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:52.060
It does not say where the balance finally settles.

43
00:02:53.133 --> 00:02:56.213
Takeaway: closing both eyes shifts the balance far less.

44
00:02:57.033 --> 00:03:00.573
So what hurt one closed eye was mainly the open eye's advantage.

45
00:03:01.633 --> 00:03:03.873
But is the amount of activity all that counts?

46
00:03:04.693 --> 00:03:06.573
The next chapter keeps the amounts equal.
